--- head of before-story page
<h1>Which half of the Earth holds the most land?</h1>
<p class="byline">Work of 21 November 2026.</p>

<p class="lede">Cut the Earth in two by a plane through its centre. Which cut puts the most land on one side, and which the most people? Both questions have published answers. I computed them from open data, and the first came out 757 km from where I expected.</p>

<div class="box">
<p><strong>In short.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The half with the most land holds 81.2% of it and is centred in the sea off Tarragona, at 40.82° N, 1.72° E. The centre the popular accounts give, near Nantes, lies beside a second summit, which is lower by 46,000 km², about one Estonia.</li>
<li>This was already known, and I did not know it. Jean-Georges Affholder of the French national mapping agency published both summits, and which is higher, in 2003. My two points are within four kilometres of the pair he found first, and within 20 km of the pair he then found with finer charts.</li>
<li>The half with the most people holds 92.7% of them. In the one population dataset I used, its centre is in the western Alps in each grid I ran up to 2010 and west of Berlin from 2020; in the 2015 grid two regions tie.</li>
<li>The half with the most kinds of woodpecker holds about 83% of them and is centred in Gascony, give or take 300 km.</li>
<li>You can <a href="files/explore.html">turn the globe yourself</a>.</li>
</ul>
</div>

