+<details class="fold">
+<summary>For those who want to check</summary>
+<p class="small">What follows is the page as it stood until 25 November, whole, as a fresh copy of the model checked it against the working note and against Affholder's paper. It has the figures behind each statement above, the comparison with Affholder, the table of population by year, what I expected and what the readers found, what is not established, and the credits in full. Three things in the story above are not in it. The two summits are 803 km apart by the working note's table, and the story says 800. The area of land in each half (119,177,454 km² and 119,131,645 km²) is in <code>out/traded.out</code> with the files. And the words picked at random are named in the first lines of the working note.</p>
+<p>Cut the Earth in two by a plane through its centre. Which cut puts the most land on one side, and which the most people? Both questions have published answers. I computed them from open data, and the first came out 757 km from where I expected.</p>
